\label{def:admissible}
{A full triangulated subcategory ${\sf A}$ of $\mathcal D$ is called
{\it right admissible} if the inclusion functor ${\sf A}\hookrightarrow \mathcal D$ has a right adjoint. Similarly, ${\sf A}$ is called {\it left
  admissible} if the inclusion functor has a left adjoint. Finally,
${\sf A}$ is {\it admissible} if it is both right and
left admissible.}

\cite[Lemma 3.1]{Bondal}\label{lem:notorious}
Let $\mathcal N\subset \mathcal D$ be a full triangulated subcategory, and consider its right orthogonal $\mathcal N ^{\perp}\subset \mathcal D$. Then the following are equivalent:

\begin{itemize}

\item[(1)] The category $\mathcal D$ is generated by $\mathcal N$ and $\mathcal N ^{\perp}$, i.e.
${\mathrm{hull}}(\mathcal N ^{\perp},\mathcal N)=\mathcal D$.

\item[(2)] For each object $x\in \mathcal D$ there exists an exact triangle $b\rightarrow x\rightarrow c$ with $b\in \mathcal N,c\in \mathcal N ^{\perp}$.

\item[(3)] The embedding functor $i_{\ast}:\mathcal N\hookrightarrow \mathcal D$ has a right adjoint $i^!:\mathcal D\rightarrow \mathcal N$.

\item[(4)] The embedding functor $j_{\ast}:\mathcal N ^{\perp}\hookrightarrow \mathcal D$ has a left adjoint $j^{\ast}:\mathcal D\rightarrow \mathcal N ^{\perp}$.

\end{itemize}

\cite[Proposition 3.2.8]{Kr}
Let $\mathcal N\subset \mathcal D$ be a thick subcategory. Then the following are equivalent:

\begin{itemize}

\item[(1)] The inclusion functor $\mathcal N\subset \mathcal D$ has a right adjoint (i.e., $\mathcal N$ is right admissible in $\mathcal D$).

\item[(2)] For each object $b\in \mathcal D$ there exists an exact triangle $a\rightarrow b\rightarrow c$ with $a\in \mathcal N,c\in \mathcal N ^{\perp}$.

\item[(3)] The canonical functor $\mathcal D \rightarrow \mathcal D /\mathcal N$ has a right adjoint.

\item[(4)] The composite $\mathcal N ^{\perp}\hookrightarrow \mathcal D \rightarrow \mathcal D/\mathcal N$ is a triangle equivalence.

\end{itemize}

